<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Weavers xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="FodyWeavers.xsd">
  <!--
    Costura.Fody is INTENTIONALLY DISABLED here.

    Why we used to embed everything:
      * ship a single .exe for ease of distribution.
      * the user double-clicks the EXE; no DLL hunt.

    Why we no longer embed (2026-06):
      Windows Defender (and several other heuristic
      AV engines) flags the embedded-payload EXE as
      Trojan:Script/Wacatac.C!ml — a known false
      positive that fires on the combination of:
        1. a single .NET EXE that contains a large
           managed payload as an embedded resource
           (Costura's hallmark);
        2. an IL-rewritten module initializer that
           registers an AssemblyResolve hook and
           calls Assembly.Load(byte[]) at startup;
        3. the loaded payload (LibreHardwareMonitorLib)
           then calls P/Invoke into ring0 / kernel32
           to load the WinRing0 driver — a third
           classic dropper-style signal.
      The Wacatac.C ML signature looks for that
      exact triple. Result: dotnet build fails with
      MSB4018 ("the file contains a virus or
      potentially unwanted software") because
      Defender locks the freshly-written EXE before
      the build pipeline can read it back to set
      version attributes.

    What we do instead:
      Ship the third-party managed DLLs as siblings
      of perfectwall-server.exe, exactly the way
      LibreHardwareMonitor ships its own helper
      binaries. The csproj's <PackageReference>s
      with PrivateAssets=All +
      CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies=true put them in
      build/ next to the EXE, and build-dotnet.ps1
      mirrors them into dist/ via its $OWN_DLLS
      allowlist.

    Trade-off accepted:
      The user now sees a folder of ~12 DLLs
      alongside perfectwall-server.exe. This is the
      standard layout for any native-friendly
      Windows system-info utility (HWiNFO, CPUID
      HWMonitor, OpenHardwareMonitor, LHM itself,
      etc.) and is the only way to get the false
      positive to go away without code-signing
      every release.
  -->
  <Costura>
    <IncludeAssemblies/>
  </Costura>
</Weavers>